(Answer: The LEGB rule defines the sequential lookup order Python uses to resolve) Tj
T*
(variable names: \(1\) Local: inside current function. \(2\) Enclosing: enclosing function) Tj
T*
(scopes from nearest to outermost. \(3\) Global: module-level variables. \(4\) Built-in:) Tj
T*
(Python core built-ins \(e.g. abs, len\). Example: x = 'Global'; def outer\(\): x =) Tj
T*
('Enclosing'; def inner\(\): x = 'Local'; print\(x\) \(prints 'Local'\). If 'Local' x is) Tj
T*
(commented out, Python checks Enclosing \('Enclosing'\), then Global \('Global'\), and) Tj
T*
(finally Built-in.) Tj
T*
